\ tools/dict-report.4 - walk the dictionary and print one line per
\ word: header bytes, body bytes, name. Used by Iteration 129 to find
\ out where a shell image's bytes actually go.
\
\ Header layout, from cross.4's "HEADER: an aligned link cell holding
\ a RELATIVE offset (previous name-field address minus this link
\ cell's own address, so it survives relocation), then a count byte
\ with bit 7 set as a marker, then the name, then alignment padding.
\ FORTH-WORDLIST holds the latest name-field address.
\
\ Body extent is computed as "start of the next-defined word's link
\ cell, minus the end of this word's name field", which works because
\ the dictionary is laid down strictly upward. The walk runs newest
\ to oldest, so the previous iteration's link cell is the current
\ word's body end.
\
\ Usage (i386 build shown; the same works for 8-byte cells):
\   printf 'S" pool.4" INCLUDED\nS" locals.4" INCLUDED\n\
\           S" shell.4" INCLUDED\nS" tools/dict-report.4" INCLUDED\n' \
\     | ./relf32 kernel32.img
\
\ Terminate on the LINK VALUE being zero, not on the computed address
\ being zero - the last word's link cell holds 0, so the computed
\ "previous" address is the link cell itself and a naive loop walks
\ off the end of the dictionary into garbage. That is what the first
\ version of this file did.

BASE @ DECIMAL
VARIABLE PREVLINK
VARIABLE NFA
VARIABLE LNK
VARIABLE GO
: CELLB 1 CELLS ;
: WALK
  HERE PREVLINK !
  FORTH-WORDLIST @ NFA !
  -1 GO !
  BEGIN GO @ WHILE
    NFA @ C@ 31 AND
    DUP 1+ ALIGNED CELLB +
    PREVLINK @  NFA @ DUP C@ 31 AND 1+ ALIGNED + -
    ." W " SWAP . . NFA @ 1+ NFA @ C@ 31 AND TYPE CR
    NFA @ CELLB - DUP PREVLINK ! @ LNK !
    LNK @ 0= IF 0 GO ! ELSE PREVLINK @ LNK @ + NFA ! THEN
  REPEAT ;
WALK
." TOTALHERE " HERE START @ - . CR
BASE ! BYE
